# Before You Tell Us to Try Again

**Historical AI recommendation.** This essay remains in the earlier recommendation or reserve record; the [later human trio](../../docs/current-selection.md) is E050, E012 and E051.


**E009 · Recommended · Assessment as of October 31, 2026**

A policy is defended by saying that people can challenge it later. But what if the policy itself takes away their evidence, resources, or ability to obtain an effective remedy? The essay follows the entire correction route under the harm that could make it necessary.

[Read the selected manuscript](../selection-20261018/E009.md) · [Read the original](original.md)

## How the argument reached this point

**September: write with two judge-profile critics.** This essay belongs to the judge-guided series. A fresh author received two distinct profile reviews before revision. Eight essays were completed from the twenty planned places; the unused places were not counted as work. [The judge experiment](../../docs/judges.md).

**October: investigate the contribution without replacing the text.** The midterm retained E009. Later literature searches examined close predecessors, while the original corpus version remained the selected manuscript. The 22 → 10 decision favoured its treatment of evidence, resources and enforcement as parts of one correction route. [Selection reasons](../../docs/selection.md).

**October 31: retain the conditional claim.** The coordinator recommended it for showing what follows when later correction is part of a policy's justification. Revisability is an established idea; the remaining question is what this account adds and how much protection is sufficient. It does not show that every policy must guarantee a successful appeal.

## What to look for while reading

Watch the difference between merely offering an appeal and preserving the means to use it after the anticipated harm. The scope of that conditional duty is the central question.

